% holding-decision: considering
% holding-decision-note: Held as a link only (README, "Cultures that protect their music"). Doreen Day and Mashkoonce "give permission for everyone to share this song", which is generous, but the song is close to ceremony (a daily offering of thanks to the water), and it is sung, not written: any score here would be our transcription of their recording. So no notation, words or audio until the family or an Anishinaabe teacher says a written version is welcome.
% holding-blocked: Still needed: written word from Doreen Day (or her family, or the Mother Earth Water Walk on her behalf) that a written score on the site is welcome and how she wants it credited; and a review by the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre, ideally with an Anishinaabe Knowledge Keeper or water walker who sings it, of the story, the teaching and who leads the singing. Neither has happened. Until then the class learns it from the Mother Earth Water Walk page and recording.
% holding-kind: link
% holding-kind-note: No notes or words by design: the song is shared orally, through its makers' own page and recording; transcribing it ourselves would put an outsider's version of a near-ceremonial song in place of theirs. Link to motherearthwaterwalk.com.
% source-url: https://www.motherearthwaterwalk.com/?p=2244 Mother Earth Water Walk, "Nibi Song": the story, the permission, the Ojibwe words and the recording
% source-url: https://www.greatersudbury.ca/live/environment-and-sustainability1/earthcare-sudbury/childrens-water-festival/water-education-videos/ka-miigwechweaanaa-nibi-let-us-give-our-thanks-to-the-water-pdf/ Rainbow District School Board and Greater Sudbury, "Ka Miigwechwe'aanaa Nibi: Let us give our thanks to the water" (2021 Water Assembly/Festival), which uses the song with credit
% performance-url: https://www.motherearthwaterwalk.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/05/02-Nibi-Song.mp3 The Nibi Song, recording hosted by the Mother Earth Water Walk (singer not named on the page)
% performance-note: The Sudbury resource says the song "is also sung by Liz Osawamick, President, Anishinaabemowin Teg"; a recording of hers, or an Anishinaabe singer visiting the class, would be the next best teacher.
% dossier-function: A song of thanks to the water, sung to it every day. The story on the Mother Earth Water Walk page, told by Beatrice Menase Kwe Jackson (Migizi Clan): Doreen Day heard Dr. Masaru Emoto say at a conference that the least we should do each day is speak to the water. Driving her grandson Mashkoonce (Little Elk) to school past a body of water, they said words of love, thanks and respect to it, and played with them in different voices. Mashkoonce asked why they could not say them in their own language, so Doreen had the words written in Ojibwemowin by her daughter's language teacher and taped them to the car visor. Then Mashkoonce suggested singing them, thinking the water would like that, and Doreen made the tune; they sang it to the water every morning on the way to school.
% dossier-elements: Not transcribed. A short song in Ojibwemowin; the page says "It is sung like a lullaby".
% dossier-practice: Sung gently, like a lullaby, to the water, every day. The Rainbow District resource invites families to give thanks to the water at sunrise and sunset; a class could sing it at the start of a water lesson or beside real water, if an Anishinaabe teacher agrees.
% dossier-accompaniment: None. The page says "we don't use shakers or drums." The Band plays nothing; no instrument of any kind is added.
% dossier-lyrics: The words are in Ojibwemowin, written for Doreen and Mashkoonce by a language teacher, and printed on the Mother Earth Water Walk page; they are not copied here. An Anishinaabemowin speaker should teach the pronunciation.
% dossier-honesty: Composed and owned: a song made by Doreen Day (Anishinaabe) at her grandson's request, not an old traditional song, though it carries Anishinaabe teaching about water. Nothing here is ours.
% dossier-licence: Terms as stated on the Mother Earth Water Walk page: "Doreen and her grandson, Mashkoonce, give permission for everyone to share this song… sing it to the water every day." This invites everyone to sing and share the song itself; it says nothing about written scores or recordings by others, and the workshop's rule is to link when unsure. The Rainbow District School Board's 2021 resource credits it as "created through the thoughtfulness of Nookomis Doreen Day at the kind request of her grandson Mashkoonce" and links to a Minnesota lesson PDF rather than printing a score. Credit wording to use: "Nibi Song, by Doreen Day (Anishinaabe), made at the request of her grandson Mashkoonce (Little Elk); story told by Beatrice Menase Kwe Jackson (Migizi Clan); shared by the Mother Earth Water Walk, motherearthwaterwalk.com" (to confirm with the family).
% dossier-respect: Close to ceremony: it is an offering of thanks to the water, sung within the Water Walk movement, in which (the Sudbury resource explains) Anishinaabe women carry the responsibility to protect the water. Always tell the story first and name Doreen Day and Mashkoonce. No shakers, drums or other instruments; no performance staging, no recording of the class for posting, and no new verses or arrangements. Ask an Anishinaabe teacher who leads it and whether it is sung at water.
% dossier-review: Doreen Day or her family (through the Mother Earth Water Walk) for any written version and the credit; the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre, with an Anishinaabe Knowledge Keeper or water walker, for classroom use. None has reviewed it.
% lesson: Before the lesson: ask the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre whether an Anishinaabe Knowledge Keeper or water walker could lead or join it, and whether tobacco should be offered; if no one can come, use the Mother Earth Water Walk page and recording.
% lesson: Tell the story first, always: Doreen Day, an Anishinaabe grandmother, drove her grandson Mashkoonce (Little Elk) to school past the water each morning, and they said words of love, thanks and respect to it. Mashkoonce asked to say them in their own language, then to sing them, because the water would like that; Doreen made the song, and they sang it to the water every day. Credit Beatrice Menase Kwe Jackson (Migizi Clan), who tells the story on the page.
% lesson: Talk about water: Anishinaabe teaching, as the Rainbow District resource explains it, gives women the responsibility to protect the water, and asks everyone to thank it. Connect this to where your school's water comes from.
% lesson: Learn the words by ear from the Mother Earth Water Walk recording, with the page's Ojibwe words projected, a line at a time; if an Anishinaabemowin speaker can visit, they teach it instead.
% lesson: Sing it softly, like a lullaby, with no shakers, drums or any instrument, as the page asks. If a Knowledge Keeper agrees, sing it outside by water, or at the start of each water lesson.
% lesson: Close: name Doreen Day and Mashkoonce again. Do not add verses, harmonies or actions, and do not record the class singing it for sharing.
